on has much stronger effective regularization, while its batch cost calculation and GeomLoss potential derivation logic align. I also found GeomLoss’ blur parameter directly controls regularization strength when using custom cost functions.\n\nI’ve confirmed the regularization strength for custom cost scenarios is √0.1 ≈ 0.316. We don’t need exact output matching, as three loose quality gates only require NRMSE ≤ 0.01, Spearman ≥ 0.999 and top-quartile overlap ≥ 0.98, leaving ample room for optimization. I’ve identified three core speedup approaches: replacing GeomLoss with a single-pass custom Sinkhorn implementation, reducing Sinkhorn iterations, and optimizing batch configuration.\n\nI’ve confirmed that larger batches reduce the number of solver pairs but increase per-solver cost, and cost matrix construction is low-overhead vectorized work. Most compute time is spent on O(N*M) Sinkhorn iterations, leading to high overhead for the current setup.\n\nI’ve confirmed we only need the dual potential output from each Sinkhorn solve to support all required calculation logic. I’ve finalized the implementation plan for v3, and will first run the v1 method to establish the optimization effect baseline before testing v3.\n\nI'll skip the v1 baseline test and directly optimize Sinkhorn solves for maximum speedup, first profiling the current canonical method's runtime to identify bottlenecks.\n"}}
